      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14184158/assets/d3de064ab8dbab633656b19cfc340f6ce43f4df8a7e18834019e6301ef67ab00.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $h$ and $x.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?",
      "worked_solution": "Since $h$ varies directly with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nh=kx.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $h=6$ and $x=3.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =k\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & =k \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nh=2x.\n$$",
